\section{The globstar \texttt{**}}

\begin{note}
The pattern \texttt{**} matches \emph{zero or more} directories,
but only when \texttt{recursive=True} is passed to \texttt{glob.glob}.
Without that parameter \texttt{**} behaves exactly like \texttt{*},
so \texttt{**/*.py} silently becomes \texttt{*/*.py} and files in the
current directory do not match.
Because \texttt{**} may match zero directories, \texttt{**/*.py} with
\texttt{recursive=True} also matches \texttt{*.py} in the top directory.
\end{note}
\begin{note}
This is called a \emph{recursive} search because internally \texttt{glob.glob} will search the files in the current directory, then recursively call itself on each of it subdirectories.
Directories form a tree structure, and so this search has exactly the same format as the search you all are implementing in your tree homeworks.
\end{note}
